(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2608318800-worldwide-real-estate-bots-record-property-into-hyakka")
  (:adr/title "Six Hermes bots record worldwide real-estate investment and purchase into wiki.kotobase.net")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-31")
  (:adr/related "adr-2608318000-hermes-residents-join-the-bots-plane")
  (:adr/body
  "## Decision

`scripts/hermes-realestate-bots/` — six scheduled Hermes bots that grow the
sourced claim graph at wiki.kotobase.net (`network-awai/app-hyakka`) with
worldwide real property, land rights, recorded transactions, official
valuations, encumbrances, real-estate investment vehicles, and the
jurisdiction-specific procedure, tax and restriction of buying property. The
ontology bot lands the vocabulary; five source bots widen it; one bounded
cloud-itonami analysis bot proposes auditable observation contracts.

| bot | daily JST | scope |
|---|---:|---|
| realestate-ontology | 00:35 | the Hyakka ontology slice, admission, query/readback |
| realestate-registry-source | 01:35 | land registries, cadastres, registrars, title, tenure |
| realestate-transaction-source | 02:35 | recorded transactions, official valuations, price indices |
| realestate-investment-source | 03:35 | REITs, funds, managers, disclosed holdings |
| realestate-procedure-source | 04:35 | purchase procedure, taxes, restrictions, permissions |
| itonami-realestate-analysis | 05:35 | cloud-itonami observation contracts |

`realestate-scope.edn` is authoritative. Corpus id `fudosan`. The
cloud-itonami candidates the analysis bot may improve are the five that exist
and were measured: real-estate, mortgage-registry, cloud-itonami-isic-6810,
cloud-itonami-isic-6820, cloud-itonami-isco-3334.

## Why this shape and not a new corpus written by hand

The family is the one ADR-2608318000 already runs: measurement, then the
model proposes, then a gate decides. `realestate_evidence.py` holds no
judgement — it syncs each bot's own worktree to origin/main, reads the tracked
text, and reports whether that bot's vocabulary tokens are present. Refusals
print a REFUSED banner and still exit 0, because Hermes injects stdout into
the prompt and an empty report reaches the model as `nothing is missing`,
which is the one answer that must never be produced by failure.

Two things were added over the startup-capital family it was modelled on.
`files_measured=` is printed, because a scan that read nothing and a scan that
read files saying nothing otherwise produce the same empty token table — the
form CLAUDE.md's six questions exist to catch. And `verify_bundle.py` checks
that every wrapper selects a scope the shared module actually defines: a
wrapper naming a scope that does not exist would refuse at argparse time,
nightly, and read as an ordinary refusal.

The ontology bot runs first in the day for the same reason the tokens exist.
Until the slice lands, `present=no` is the honest answer, and rule 1 of every
source prompt turns that into a stop.

## Why the epistemic boundaries are the substance

Property data is unusually good at producing a number that is true of
something and is then read as a number about the property in front of you. The
scope names each conflation and the prompts carry the sentence:

- a listing asking price is not a transaction price;
- a recorded transaction price is not current market value;
- an official or assessed valuation is a tax figure, not a market price;
- a price index is not a property-level price and cannot be applied to one;
- an automated valuation is not a verified valuation;
- registered title is not beneficial ownership;
- a mortgage record is not current debt outstanding;
- a portfolio disclosure is an observed claim at its reporting date;
- a zoning designation is not permission to build, and a planning permission
  is not a completed building;
- areas differ by measurement standard, amounts are nominal at their own date,
  and neither is comparable without a stated basis;
- missing is unmeasured, and worldwide is a coverage goal, not a completeness
  claim.

## Why the privacy boundary is stricter than the sources

Land registries in several jurisdictions publish the names of individual
owners. That a source publishes something does not make it admissible here:
this plane releases claims under CC0, irrevocably, and a CC0 claim naming a
natural person as the owner of a dwelling cannot be withdrawn. So the corpus
holds legal entities and public professional roles only — no natural-person
owner identification, no personal residential-address linkage, no occupancy or
household data, no mortgage-borrower identity, no personal wealth, and no
neighbourhood desirability ranking. A property address is a property of the
property; a person attached to it is not.

This is written as corpus policy rather than left as an omission, because an
omission with no reason attached gets filled in by the next person who notices
it is missing.

## The gate was made to fail before it was landed

`verify_bundle.py` was landed only after being made to fail three ways, each
report naming exactly the thing broken: a dropped privacy boundary
(`realestate-scope.edn: missing :no-natural-person-owner-identification`), a
softened price-index sentence in the transaction prompt, and a wrapper
mistyped to select a scope the shared module does not define. Restored, it
passes. Neither direction was assumed.

## Measured at landing (2026-08-31)

- Six cron jobs created and listed, next fires 2026-09-01 00:35–05:35 JST.
- Six isolated worktrees created; the five hyakka ones under ~/.gftd/worktrees
  because app-hyakka's deps.edn resolves ../../kotoba-lang/kotobase-client/src
  through ~/.gftd/kotoba-lang, and npm install completed in each.
- All six evidence scripts run, exit 0. The five hyakka scopes measure 72
  tracked files each and report their tokens absent, which is correct before
  the ontology bot has run. The analysis scope measures 3,725 files and finds
  all five cloud-itonami candidates present.
- `verify_bundle.py` passes: 6 prompts, 6 isolated evidence scopes, 11 scope
  boundaries.

## The model, measured twice on the day it landed

At install the bots were left on `murakumo-main`, because
`hyakka-model-refresh` REFUSED: a Hermes config-format migration had added
top-level keys the resolver does not own (`_config_version`, `agent`,
`plugins`), and it left every bot's model exactly as it was — the correct
behaviour, and the reason the refusal was visible at all. `murakumo-main` was
probed rather than assumed (HTTP 200, alias-for `qwen3.8-27b`, status
`serving`), so the fallback would have carried them.

By closing the same day, another session's `carry-forward` fix for that
refusal had been installed into `~/.hermes/scripts/resolve_free_model.cljs`
and the refresh had run. Measured from `~/.hermes/cron/jobs.json`: all six
jobs are on `z-ai/glm-5.3-flash` / `openrouter-free`. Nobody named them —
the refresh covers every agent-driven job by default, which is exactly why a
name list is worse than none: it does not fail when a bot it has never heard
of appears.

Two things are worth keeping from that sequence. The refusal was a
measurement and was reported as one, so the ADR did not have to be wrong to
be current. And the fix is installed but is still not committed to `main`,
so the reviewable original under `scripts/hermes-hyakka-bots/` and the copy
Hermes actually runs have drifted — the hazard that directory's README names
about itself.

## Consequences

- The corpus grows only where a first-party authority publishes. Commercial
  aggregators, brokerage portals and news are discovery-only; search snippets,
  scraped directories and inferred ownership or valuation are forbidden.
- No bot gives investment advice, values a property, ranks a market, bids,
  offers, trades, allocates capital, contacts a party or makes a financial
  commitment. Each takes at most two sources and two jurisdictions per run and
  opens at most one PR.
- Opening no PR is a correct run.
- The corpus itself does not exist yet. The ontology bot proposes it through
  app-hyakka's own gate; this ADR does not pre-empt that decision, and a
  hand-written corpus namespace here would have.")))
